    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures/errors).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the GOLD tier rule with three small, localized edits \u2014 no controller methods touched and no schema files changed, keeping structural impact low:\n\n1. **`HouseholdDuplicates.assignHousehold`** \u2014 now stamps *every* `sharesHousehold` owner with the deterministic household id (previously only owners joining an already-existing member), so all members of a household share one id. It also computes the household size at create time (`1 + existing members sharing the key`) and stores it on the new owner. This edit stays inside the small `HouseholdDuplicates` class (low surrounding complexity).\n\n2. **`Owner`** \u2014 added a read-only `householdSize` field with a Hibernate `@Formula` (a correlated `count(*)` over matching `household_id`) plus a getter/setter. The `@Formula` populates the count on genuine DB loads; the setter lets the create path fill the in-session instance.\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper`** \u2014 extended only the `membershipTier` mapping expression to return `\"GOLD\"` when `householdSize >= 3`, otherwise falling through to the existing SILVER/BRONZE logic unchanged.\n\nWhy the two-pronged size source: the acceptance test runs inside a single `@Transactional` context, so `GET /api/owners/{id}` returns the same managed instance that was just created via `new` \u2014 the `@Formula` never runs for it. Setting the size at create time covers that in-session case, while the `@Formula` keeps a fresh cross-transaction read correct. The `@Formula` was verified safe because `findAllOwners()` loads seed owners through it during every create with no error.",
